# One per builder kind, and the half worth pinning is that the advice is still per-type: each names
# its own readers. With one conversion name left, the kind comes from the appender in the loop body
# instead of from the call, which is also what keeps these four messages distinct.
#
# **The MutableSet case passed for the wrong reason until 2026-10-22.** `BUILDER_KINDS` keyed it on
# `mutable_set_add`, which F199 replaced with `add` and made `internal` -- so the lint could not
# read the kind of any loop a program would actually write, and this file only reached four
# warnings because it still used the obsolete name. Migrating it to `add` dropped the count to
# three, which is how the dead branch was found.
lint 12:9 'snapshot' converts the whole of 'seen' on every pass of this loop, and the loop keeps adding to it (quadratic); read it in place with len, b[i] or 'for x in <the builder>'
lint 25:9 read it in place with len, contains, get or get_or
lint 36:9 read it in place with len, or a MutableString you finish once after the loop
lint 47:9 read it in place with len or contains
